values
  ('when-your-child-is-frightened-by-the-news', 'When Your Child Is Frightened by the News', 'Questions at bedtime about war or the climate. How to find out what they actually heard, tell the truth in their size, and give them something to do about it.', 'They heard something on the radio in the car, or saw a headline over your shoulder, or somebody at school said something. And now there are questions at bedtime about war, or the climate, or whether it could happen here.

You want to tell them it is fine. You also do not want to lie to them. Those feel like the only two options and there is a better one in between.

Find out what they actually heard

Answer the question they asked rather than the one you are imagining, because children usually know less and worry about something more specific than you expect.

So ask first. What did you hear. What do you think is happening. What is the bit that worries you most.

Very often the worry is concrete and fixable: that it is happening on our street, that it is happening now, that you will have to go, that they will be alone. Those can be answered plainly, and the general dread cannot.

Tell them the truth in their size

Children can handle true things said simply. What frightens them is sensing that something is wrong and being told nothing.

So give the honest, small version. Yes, there is a war, and it is a long way from here. Yes, the planet is getting warmer, and a lot of grown-ups whose whole job this is are working on it.

Then the three things they actually want to know, said out loud whether or not they ask: you are safe, I am not going anywhere, and it is my job to look after you, not yours.

Do not promise what you cannot promise. Nothing bad will ever happen is a promise that gets found out. I will always tell you the truth and I will always look after you is one you can keep.

Match the detail to the age

A four-year-old needs almost nothing: something sad happened far away, you are safe, shall we read this.

A seven or eight-year-old wants facts and a map. Where is it, how far, who is helping.

A teenager has already seen more than you have, probably in video form, and needs a conversation rather than reassurance. They will respect I find it frightening too, and here is what I do with that, far more than it is all under control.

Turn the volume down at home

A great deal of this is logistics rather than conversation.

So notice what is on in the background. Rolling news in the kitchen, the radio on the school run, your own phone in front of them. Children absorb the tone more than the content.

Check their own feed if they have one, not as surveillance but as a conversation. What are you seeing. That is worth knowing about, since the algorithm does not care how old they are.

And keep your own reaction in view. If they see you grim and scrolling at the table, that tells them more than anything you say.

Give them something to do

Helplessness is most of what makes this heavy, for children even more than adults, and the antidote is small and concrete action.

Collect something for a food bank. Pick up litter on the walk. Write a card. Give to a collection with their own money. Plant something. Learn where their water comes from.

It will not fix the problem and it is not supposed to. It teaches them that frightening things are things people do something about, which is the most useful thing they can learn from this.

Protect the ordinary

The most stabilizing thing in a frightening week is a normal Tuesday.

So keep the routines going. Dinner at the usual time, the usual bedtime, swimming on Thursday, the same jokes. Children read safety off the pattern of the day rather than off your explanations.

Bedtime is where this tends to surface, so leave a slot earlier in the evening for the big questions, and keep the last twenty minutes dull and warm.

Watch for the signs it has gone deeper

Most worry about the news passes in days. Sometimes it settles in, and that is worth catching early.

Signs: not sleeping or coming into your bed again, stomach aches and headaches, not wanting to go to school, checking the news repeatedly, asking the same question over and over however many times you answer, or going quiet about it altogether after a period of asking.

Answering the same question for the fifteenth time is the one most parents miss. If reassurance is not sticking, more reassurance is not the answer and something else is going on.

When to get help

Please talk to your doctor or your child''s school if the worry has lasted more than a few weeks, if they are not sleeping, if they are refusing school, or if reassurance stops working no matter how often you give it, because anxiety in children is common, well understood and treatable, often in a short course of sessions with a professional who works with young people. Schools usually have somebody or know who to ask, and that is a reasonable first call. And look after yourself too: if the news has your own sleep or mood down, a therapist or your own doctor is worth it, partly for you and partly because a steady adult is most of what a frightened child is reading.', array['Parenting', 'Anxiety']::text[], 0, '2026-11-03T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('napping-without-wrecking-your-night', 'Napping Without Wrecking Your Night', 'Naps have a bad reputation they half deserve. Why twenty minutes or ninety work and forty-five does not, when in the day to take one, and who should not be napping at all.', 'You are wiped out at three in the afternoon. There is a sofa. And somewhere in your head is a rule that says napping will ruin tonight, so you push through instead, and then you are useless until six and still not sleeping well.

Naps have a bad reputation they half deserve. Done badly they do wreck the night. Done with a little thought they are one of the most useful things available to a tired person.

What actually makes a nap backfire

Two things, and they are both about timing rather than the nap itself.

The first is length. Sleep runs in cycles, and surfacing from the deep middle of one leaves you groggy, heavy and worse than before. That fog is where the nap gets blamed, and it is a landing problem rather than a sleep problem.

The second is lateness. The sleepiness that builds through the day is what gets you off at bedtime. A long nap at six in the evening spends that, and then you lie there at eleven wide awake, and conclude that naps are not for you.

Keep it to twenty minutes, or go long on purpose

Those are the two versions that work, and the middle ground is the one to avoid.

Twenty minutes, or anything up to about twenty-five, keeps you in light sleep. You wake quickly, you feel better within a few minutes, and your night is almost untouched. This is the one to use on an ordinary tired afternoon.

Ninety minutes gets you through a full cycle and out the other side, which also lands cleanly, but it is a real chunk of sleep and it will affect your night. Save that for a genuine deficit, like after a bad night with a baby or a night shift.

Forty-five to sixty minutes is the version that leaves people feeling terrible, and it is the length most accidental naps turn out to be.

Set an alarm. Nobody wakes themselves at twenty minutes reliably, and the fear of oversleeping is itself enough to stop you dropping off.

Do it early enough

The general rule is before the middle of your afternoon, and well before seven hours ahead of your bedtime.

For most people that means between about one and three in the afternoon, which happens to be when the natural dip in alertness arrives anyway. You are not fighting your body at that hour, which is why those naps come easily.

After about four, you are borrowing from tonight. If you are desperate later than that, keep it to ten or fifteen minutes.

Make the twenty minutes work

A short nap is more about letting go than sleeping deeply, and a few small things help.

Somewhere dim, an eye mask, something over you, because body temperature drops as you fall asleep and being slightly cold keeps you up.

Not your bed, if you have insomnia at night. Keeping the bed for nighttime sleep matters, and a chair or a sofa is better for you.

A coffee immediately before, if you need to be sharp afterward, which sounds backward and works, because caffeine takes about twenty minutes to arrive and lands as you wake.

And do not chase it. If sleep does not come, lying still with your eyes shut for twenty minutes does more for you than another hour of pushing on. Rest counts even without sleep.

Know whether you should be napping at all

This is the honest caveat, because for one group of people naps make things worse.

If you lie awake at night, if getting to sleep is the problem, then daytime sleep is working against you, and the standard advice for insomnia is to stop napping while you get the nights back. That is hard and it is the right order to do it in.

If you sleep fine at night and are still exhausted all day, that is worth a doctor rather than a nap. Needing to sleep in the afternoon every day, falling asleep without meaning to, or waking unrefreshed however long you sleep are all things with causes worth finding.

Stop treating it as a moral question

A lot of the trouble here is not physiological. It is the belief that a nap is laziness, which means people either refuse one they need or take one and feel bad for the rest of the day.

A twenty-minute nap is a tool, in the same category as a glass of water. It makes the afternoon better, it makes you less irritable with the people around you, and it is cheaper than the three coffees that are the usual alternative.

When to get help

Please see your doctor if you are sleepy every day no matter how much you sleep, if you fall asleep without intending to, if you snore heavily or wake gasping, or if you need naps to get through a day that used to be fine without them, because those point to things that are diagnosable and treatable rather than to a character flaw. Say it plainly if your mood has been low alongside the tiredness, since the two travel together often. And if the nights are the real problem, ask specifically about treatment for insomnia, which works well and is not primarily about medication.', array['Sleep', 'Habits']::text[], 0, '2026-11-03T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-motivation-comes-in-bursts', 'When Motivation Comes in Bursts', 'Three unstoppable weeks, then a month of nothing. Why the two halves are one pattern, what to spend a burst on instead of promises, and the signs it needs more than better planning.', 'For three weeks you are unstoppable. You are up early, the project is moving, the house is clean, you have started running. Then something tips, and for the next month you cannot make yourself answer an email.

Then the story you tell about it: that the good weeks are the real you and the rest is laziness. That story is both wrong and the main thing keeping the cycle going.

Look at the shape rather than the weeks

The useful move is to stop judging each stretch separately and look at the pattern, because they are not two separate things. They are one thing with two halves.

The burst is usually everything at once: all the projects, no rest days, late nights, enormous enthusiasm, new commitments made to other people.

Then the crash: nothing, dread about what you promised, avoidance of the people you promised it to, and a flat stretch that is treated as evidence about your character.

Seen as a cycle, the crash stops looking like a failure of will. It looks like what happens after three weeks of running something at a level it cannot be run at.

Find out what starts the burst

These rarely come from nowhere. There is usually a trigger, and knowing it helps.

Often it is relief: a deadline passed, an illness over, a decision made. Sometimes it is a fresh start, a new year, a new job, a new plan. Sometimes it is anxiety, which can look exactly like productivity for a while.

And sometimes it follows a crash, because a bad month builds up a debt of things undone, and the burst is an attempt to clear it in a week.

Notice which of those yours is and you can see the next one coming.

Spend the burst differently

You are not going to stop having energetic stretches, and you should not want to. The thing to change is what you do with them.

So do not use the burst to make promises. That is the single most expensive habit in this pattern: committing in week two to things that have to be delivered in week six, when you are flat.

Use it instead to build the floor. Set things up so the lean weeks are survivable. Batch the cooking, do the admin, set up the automatic payments, write the thing down so your tired self does not have to remember it, say no to the extra commitment.

And rest during the good weeks, which feels absurd and is the whole intervention. A day off in week two is what makes week five possible.

Build a floor you can hold on the worst week

The usual plan is written during the burst and sized for the burst, which is why it collapses.

So set the version you could do on a flat day. Not the gym, a ten-minute walk. Not the chapter, a paragraph. Not the deep clean, the dishes.

Then do that in the good weeks too, as the baseline, and treat anything above it as a bonus rather than the new standard. Keeping the floor through the lean stretch is what stops it becoming a true crash, because the hardest thing about the flat weeks is restarting from zero.

Treat the flat stretch as the predictable half

When it arrives, the response matters more than you would think.

So skip the self-prosecution. The lecture about wasted potential does not produce work and does reliably extend the flat stretch, because shame is exhausting.

Do the floor, keep eating and sleeping and going outside, tell one person you are in a low patch, and let the week be unremarkable. Most people find the flat stretch shortens considerably once they stop fighting it.

And do not cancel everything. Keeping one or two commitments through it is what keeps you connected to the world.

Watch for the version that needs more than this

Most of this is an ordinary pattern of overdoing and recovering, and some of it is not.

If the high stretches come with very little sleep and no tiredness, racing speech, spending you regret, risky decisions or feeling unusually powerful, that is a different thing and worth a professional''s eye rather than better planning.

Likewise if the flat stretches come with weeks of hopelessness, no interest in anything, or sleeping far more or far less than usual.

When to get help

Please talk to your doctor if the swings are large, if the high stretches involve barely sleeping while feeling great, or if the low stretches last weeks and come with hopelessness, because the pattern you are describing may be something specific and treatable rather than a problem with your discipline, and that is worth finding out properly. A therapist can help with the cycle itself, particularly the part where the burst is driven by anxiety or by making up for lost ground. And if the lean weeks are getting longer or heavier each time, do not wait for a good stretch to ask, because the good stretch is when you will decide you no longer need to.', array['Motivation', 'Burnout']::text[], 0, '2026-11-03T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-a-decision-has-been-made-for-you', 'When a Decision Has Been Made for You', 'The job is going, the move is happening, the answer was no. Why losing your say hurts more than the outcome, where the anger belongs, and which margins are still yours.', 'The decision has been made. The job is going. The treatment is the one they recommend. The move is happening because of somebody else''s job. The sale went through. The answer was no and there is no appeal.

You were not consulted, or you were consulted and overruled, and now you have to live inside an outcome you did not pick. That is a specific kind of difficult, and it is not the same as making a hard choice.

Name what is actually hard about it

People in this position often cannot work out why they feel so bad, because on paper the new arrangement may be fine.

It is rarely the outcome. It is the loss of say. Being able to influence what happens to you is something people need at a fairly deep level, and losing it produces a reaction out of proportion to how bad the outcome is.

So you can be genuinely all right about the thing itself and still angry, flat and unsettled for weeks. That is not ingratitude or inconsistency. It is a response to having had no vote.

Let the anger be about the right thing

The anger will find a target, and left alone it usually picks the nearest person rather than the right one.

So be specific with yourself. I am furious that this was decided in a meeting I was not in. I am angry that I found out by email. I am angry that nobody asked what it would do to my week.

Said precisely, it stops leaking onto your partner, the person who delivered the news, or the colleague who had nothing to do with it.

And allow that it may be legitimate. Not every imposed decision is reasonable, and the fact that it is final does not make it right. You can think it was handled badly and still have to live with it.

Separate the parts that are still yours

Almost no decision takes everything. The useful work is finding the margins that remain, because that is where your sense of agency comes back.

Not whether you move, but which neighborhood, and what you take. Not whether the job ends, but how you leave, what you say, what you do next. Not whether you have the treatment, but who comes with you, what you ask, what you want to know.

Write the two lists if it helps. Decided for me. Still mine. The second one is usually longer than it feels, and working on it does more for how you feel than any amount of arguing about the first.

Ask the questions you are entitled to

Even a final decision usually has information attached, and getting it is both practical and steadying.

Why this. Who decided. What happens next and when. What are my options within it. What would it take to revisit it, and is it genuinely closed.

Ask once, plainly, and in writing where that is appropriate. The answer is often less arbitrary than it looked, and sometimes the margins are wider than you were told.

Do not spend months on the appeal in your head

There is a stage where you argue the case endlessly to yourself, assemble the points you should have made, and replay the meeting.

That is normal for a week or two. Past that it costs you the energy you need for the part you can affect.

It helps to say the plain sentence out loud. This has been decided, I did not choose it, and I am going to spend my effort on what happens now. Not as acceptance exactly, more as a decision about where your attention goes.

If there is a real appeal available, make it properly and then put it down until there is an answer.

Mark the thing you lost

An imposed decision often takes something real: a house, a role, a plan, a version of the next five years.

That deserves an acknowledgment rather than being skipped over because the new arrangement is workable. Say what you are losing, to somebody. Walk the route one more time. Keep something.

People who do this move on better than those who go straight to being reasonable about it, because the reasonableness arrives faster when the loss has been admitted.

When to get help

Please talk to a therapist if the anger or the flatness is still running your weeks a couple of months on, if you are not sleeping, or if this has stirred up an older experience of having no say, which is very often what makes one of these land so hard. See your doctor if your mood has been low for weeks or the stress is showing up in your body, and say plainly that something was decided over your head, because it is a real cause rather than a trivial one. And if the decision is a medical one you are uneasy about, a second opinion is a reasonable thing to ask for rather than a complaint about the first.', array['Decision Making', 'Emotion Regulation']::text[], 0, '2026-11-03T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('the-thoughts-that-arrive-uninvited', 'The Thoughts That Arrive Uninvited', 'A disturbing thought appears fully formed and you tell nobody for years. Why almost everyone has them, why they are not wishes, and why fighting them is what makes them stick.', 'You are holding a knife at the kitchen counter and a thought arrives about using it. You are at the top of the stairs with the baby and your mind produces an image of dropping her. You are in a meeting and something obscene appears, fully formed, about the person speaking.

Then the second wave, which is worse than the thought: what kind of person thinks that. And you tell nobody, possibly for years.

Almost everybody has them

This is the piece of information that changes the most for people, so it is worth stating plainly. Unwanted, disturbing, out-of-character thoughts are a normal feature of human minds. Violent ones, sexual ones, blasphemous ones, ones about harm coming to people you love.

Minds generate a constant stream of material, most of it discarded before you notice. Some of it is strange. The difference between people is not whether these arrive but how much weight gets put on them.

New parents get them constantly, often about harm coming to the baby, which is both terrifying and extremely common. So do people who are exhausted, stressed or unwell.

They are not wishes

The reason these thoughts frighten you so much is that you are treating them as evidence about what you want. They are not.

In fact the pattern runs the other way. The thoughts that stick are the ones most opposed to what you care about, because those are the ones that generate alarm, and alarm is what makes a thought memorable and repeat.

A person who genuinely wanted to harm somebody would not be horrified by the thought. The horror is the proof that this is not who you are, which is exactly backward from how it feels.

Watch what the fighting does

Here is the mechanism that keeps people stuck. The thought arrives, it frightens you, so you try very hard not to have it.

Trying not to think something tells your mind that the thought matters, and your mind supplies more of what matters. The effort is the fuel.

So are the things you do to feel better: checking, avoiding the stairs, not holding the knife, not being alone with the child, mentally replacing the bad image with a good one, asking somebody to tell you you are not dangerous. Each of those brings relief for a few minutes and makes the next thought stickier, because it confirms the thought was a threat worth managing.

Change how you hold it rather than whether it comes

You cannot stop the thoughts arriving. You can change what happens in the next thirty seconds.

Name it flatly. That is an intrusive thought. There it is again.

Let it be there without arguing with it, agreeing with it, or analyzing where it came from. The analysis is another form of grappling.

Then carry on with what your hands were doing. Keep chopping. Keep walking down the stairs. Staying put is the part that teaches your mind the thought is not an emergency.

Expect it to feel worse before it feels better, because not performing the usual reassurance is uncomfortable the first several times.

Say it out loud to one person

The secrecy is doing more damage than the thoughts. People carry these for decades, convinced they are uniquely monstrous, and the moment of saying it to somebody is usually the turning point.

A professional is the best choice, because they will not be surprised and will know exactly what you are describing. A trusted person is also fine.

Keep in mind that this is not the same as asking for reassurance on a loop. Saying it once, properly, to be understood, is different from needing to hear you are safe fifteen times a day.

The honest line about safety

It is worth being clear, because people in this position worry about exactly this. Thoughts you find horrifying and do not want to act on are not the same as intentions, and having them does not make you dangerous.

What is different is a thought you find yourself drawn to, or a plan, or an urge that feels like something you might do. That is a reason to tell somebody today rather than to sit with it, and it is a different conversation from this one.

When to get help

Please talk to a therapist if these thoughts are taking up real time, if you are avoiding people or places because of them, or if you have built a set of rituals to keep them away, because this is one of the best understood and most treatable things a professional sees, and the treatment is specific rather than general talking. Tell your doctor too if you have had a baby recently and the thoughts are about the baby, since that is common and there is good help for it and nobody will think you are a danger to your child for saying so. And if you ever notice a thought you feel pulled toward rather than frightened by, please tell someone the same day.', array['Thinking Traps', 'Shame']::text[], 0, '2026-11-03T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
